# what the console writes while it runs
console_state.json
*.vrset
launch_err.txt

# A real site's printout and the backup behind it. Same rule as
# tests/real_backups/: the data stays on the machine that has it and never
# goes into the repository, because it carries the site's address and its
# account number. tests/test_tape.py skips when it is not there.
tests/tape/

# One developer's tool settings, not the project's. Committed by accident
# once and taken back out; it holds nothing secret, it just is not the
# repository's business which permissions somebody's editor was given.
# By BASENAME rather than by path, so the rule does not have to spell the
# tool's name -- which is a string the public snapshot refuses to carry,
# and this file ships.
settings.local.json

# python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
build/
dist/

# virtualenvs
.venv/
venv/
env/

# scratch state the tests and screenshots leave behind
tests/_*.json
*.log

# editors and operating systems
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# the manuals this was built from: large, and Veeder-Root's to distribute
reference/*.pdf
# and text pulled out of them: same documents, same reason
reference/*.txt

# photographs of real hardware: reference only, not ours to distribute
reference_console.png
reference_keypad.png

# generated by packaging/make_version_info.py from __version__
packaging/version_info.txt

# real console backups a developer drops in to replay; never committed
tests/real_backups/

# stray editor file
New Text Document*.txt

# a sync client writes these beside the file it could not merge, and one
# got committed as a 5,291 line copy of wire.py
*(# Edit conflict *#)*
# the same client's other name for the same thing. It made three of these
# while xport.py was being written, and each one silently took some of the
# edits meant for the real file: a change lands in the copy, the file keeps
# the old text, and the tests pass on neither.
*(# Name clash *#)*

# a capture taken off a real TCP/IP card, for tests/test_xport_capture.py.
# Same rule as reference/*.pdf: it is the device's own output and it takes a
# card to make. tools/capture_xport.py remakes it; the test skips without it.
tests/xport_capture/

# a capture taken off a real TLS-350 through its TCP/IP card, for checking
# the console emulation against hardware. Same rule as reference/*.pdf and
# tests/xport_capture/: it is the console's own output and it takes a console
# to make. tools/capture_console.py remakes it; the tests skip without it.
tests/console_capture/
